[Event "Study line 02-01"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "The normal Carlsbad setup"]
[Result "*"]

{ cxd5 fixes a structure you can study repeatedly. Bg5 develops outside the e3 pawn chain; Bd3 points at h7 while supporting later central play. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 Be7 5. cxd5 exd5 6. Bg5 O-O 7. e3 c6 8. Bd3 Nbd7 9. O-O Re8 10. Qc2 Nf8 { Complete development with a rook on b1 or c1 according to Black's setup. Prepare b4-b5, and meet a kingside initiative with exchanges or a central break. Watch for: The bishop sacrifice Bxh7+ is not justified merely because your bishop points at h7. Check the king's escape squares and whether Ng5 is effective. } *

[Event "Study line 02-02"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "Black exchanges the g5 bishop"]
[Result "*"]

{ Bh4 keeps the pin available; when the knight reaches e4, exchanging on e7 simplifies the pressure on your bishop without abandoning development. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 Be7 5. cxd5 exd5 6. Bg5 h6 7. Bh4 O-O 8. e3 Ne4 9. Bxe7 Qxe7 10. Nxd5 Qd8 { In this exact position, Nxd5 takes a pawn that Black no longer adequately protects. Continue development while meeting any queen or knight activity. Watch for: Do not copy Nxd5 into a position where Black has already played ...c6. That extra pawn would control d5. } *

[Event "Study line 02-03"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "Early ...c5 changes the structure"]
[Result "*"]

{ Black challenges d4 immediately. Keep developing while monitoring the tension rather than forcing a minority-attack script onto the position. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 Be7 5. cxd5 exd5 6. Bg5 c5 7. e3 Nc6 8. dxc5 O-O 9. Bb5 h6 10. Bh4 Bxc5 { After exchanges on c5 or d4, identify whose d-pawn is isolated. Blockade an enemy isolated pawn with pieces; use activity if the isolated pawn is yours. Watch for: A rook on b1 can be pointless when the main battle has moved to the central files. } *

[Event "Study line 02-04"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "The bishop comes to f5 too soon"]
[Result "*"]

{ Exchanging the f6 knight removes a defender of d5. This concrete detail makes automatic e3 less ambitious in this exact position. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 Be7 5. cxd5 exd5 6. Bg5 Bf5 7. Bxf6 Bxf6 8. Qb3 c6 9. Qxb7 Qb6 10. Qxb6 axb6 { Inspect d5 and b7 after Black recaptures. Queen pressure on the queenside may force concessions before you complete normal development. Watch for: This tactic depends on Black not yet having played ...c6 or castled. Check the exact position before repeating it. } *

[Event "Study line 02-05"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "Black develops ...Nbd7 before ...Be7"]
[Result "*"]

{ This move order reaches the same familiar structure. Recognizing the transposition saves unnecessary memorization. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 Nbd7 5. cxd5 exd5 6. Bg5 Be7 7. e3 O-O 8. Bd3 c6 9. O-O Ne8 10. Bxe7 Qxe7 { Qc2, Rab1 and b4 are possible preparations. Keep an eye on ...Ne4 and ...Re8 before committing. Watch for: Transpositions depend on the exact piece placement and whose turn it is. Similar pawn structures alone do not prove two positions are identical. } *

[Event "Study line 03-01"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "Ragozin: exchange first"]
[Result "*"]

{ The exchange removes the tension before the pin generates more complications. Bg5 counter-pins the f6 knight and e3 prepares Bd3. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 Bb4 5. cxd5 exd5 6. Bg5 h6 7. Bh4 O-O 8. e3 Bf5 9. Be2 Nbd7 10. Qb3 c5 { Develop Bd3 and castle. If ...Bxc3 damages your pawns, compare the bishop pair and central control with the structural cost. Watch for: Do not forget ...g5 can drive the bishop while ...Ne4 creates central pressure. } *

[Event "Study line 03-02"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "Vienna: accept the central invitation"]
[Result "*"]

{ e4 uses the tempo Black spent capturing c4. The center gives space but becomes the target of ...c5 and ...Nxe4 ideas. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 dxc4 5. e4 Bb4 6. Bg5 h6 7. Bxf6 Qxf6 8. Bxc4 c5 9. O-O cxd4 10. e5 Qd8 { Support the center, develop actively, and calculate before recovering c4. A pawn returned for a lead in development can be a good bargain. Watch for: The c3 knight may be pinned. Recount e4 defenders after ...Bb4 before treating the pawn as safe. } *

[Event "Study line 03-03"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "Janowski: ...a6"]
[Result "*"]

{ Black has invested a tempo in ...a6. You can enter an Exchange structure without attempting an immediate punishment. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 a6 4. Nc3 Nf6 5. cxd5 exd5 6. Bg5 Be7 7. e3 h6 8. Bh4 c6 9. Qc2 O-O 10. Bd3 Be6 { Develop and castle. ...a6 may make a later b4-b5 break harder or prepare ...b5, so queenside play needs a fresh calculation. Watch for: A flank move is not automatically a blunder, and a premature attack can give back your development lead. } *

[Event "Study line 03-04"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "An early queen check on a5"]
[Result "*"]

{ Bd2 unpins the knight and develops a piece. Black's queen sortie gives you a concrete developing response. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 c5 5. cxd5 Nxd5 6. e3 Qa5 7. Bd2 Qd8 8. Nxd5 exd5 9. Bb5+ Bd7 10. Bxd7+ Qxd7 { Bd3 and castling are the priorities. Treat any c-file exchanges as a route into isolated-pawn positions. Watch for: Do not play a center move that leaves your c3 knight undefended along the a5-e1 diagonal. } *

[Event "Study line 03-05"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "Black plays ...h6 very early"]
[Result "*"]

{ Bf4 develops to a useful square without arguing with ...h6. The Exchange structure can still be reached. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 h6 4. Nc3 Nf6 5. cxd5 exd5 6. Bf4 Bd6 7. Bxd6 Qxd6 8. Qc2 O-O 9. e3 a6 10. h3 b6 { Use e3, Bd3 and O-O. Depending on Black's deployment, place the queen on c2 and coordinate the rooks. Watch for: Do not spend multiple moves trying to prove that ...h6 was a waste. Your own development is the practical gain. } *

[Event "Study line 03-06"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "Triangle move order: choose e3 before Nc3"]
[Result "*"]

{ With ...e6 and ...c6 already played, e3 prepares to recover c4 without entering a Noteboom pawn-grab line with the b1 knight committed too early. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 c6 4. e3 Nf6 5. Nc3 Nbd7 6. Bd3 dxc4 7. Bxc4 b5 8. Bd3 Bb7 9. e4 b4 10. Na4 c5 { If Black develops ...Nf6, return to the e3 Semi-Slav. If ...dxc4 comes first, recover with Bxc4 and watch ...b5. Watch for: Playing Nc3 automatically against the triangle allows ...dxc4 and a different body of theory. } *

[Event "Study line 03-07"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "Triangle: Black takes on c4 immediately"]
[Result "*"]

{ The e3 pawn move allowed a clean bishop recapture. Black can still expand, but White develops toward a central response. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 c6 4. e3 dxc4 5. Bxc4 b5 6. Bd3 Nf6 7. O-O a6 8. a4 Bb7 9. Nbd2 Nbd7 10. Nb3 Be7 { Develop the b1 knight and prepare e4 when ready. This is related to the Semi-Slav structures in Booklet 06. Watch for: Remember to retreat the bishop when ...b5 attacks it. } *

[Event "Study line 04-01"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "Classical development"]
[Result "*"]

{ e3 opens the bishop and Bxc4 recovers the pawn with development. Qe2 and Rd1 build pressure on the central file. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. e3 e6 5. Bxc4 c5 6. O-O a6 7. Qe2 b5 8. Bb3 Bb7 9. Rd1 Nbd7 10. e4 cxd4 { Develop the b1 knight to c3 or d2 according to tactics. Watch ...cxd4 and decide which piece or pawn should recapture. Watch for: Do not push d5 just because the rook is on d1; make sure the resulting exchanges do not lose the center. } *

[Event "Study line 04-02"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "Black tries to hold with ...b5"]
[Result "*"]

{ a4 attacks the base of Black's chain. After ...b4, e3 opens lines and prepares to recover c4. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. Nf3 b5 4. a4 b4 5. e3 Nf6 6. Bxc4 e6 7. a5 a6 8. O-O Bb7 9. Nbd2 Be7 10. Re1 O-O { Develop Bxc4 when safe, castle, and exploit the queenside weaknesses. Material is less important than getting your pieces out while Black spends pawn moves. Watch for: After ...b4, a knight developed automatically to c3 can be attacked with tempo. } *

[Event "Study line 04-03"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "An immediate ...c5"]
[Result "*"]

{ The same development scheme works. The key is that your e-pawn is still available to support or recapture in the center. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. Nf3 c5 4. e3 Nf6 5. Bxc4 e6 6. O-O a6 7. a4 cxd4 8. exd4 Nc6 9. Nc3 Be7 10. Be3 O-O { Nc3 and Qe2 are useful, with the d-file becoming important after central exchanges. Watch for: An early dxc5 can leave e4 available to Black and should not be played by habit. } *

[Event "Study line 04-04"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "Black prepares ...b5 with ...a6"]
[Result "*"]

{ a4 immediately asks whether Black can hold the extra pawn without concessions. Trading the a-rooks can leave b5 and c4 as targets. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. Nf3 a6 4. e3 b5 5. a4 Bb7 6. axb5 axb5 7. Rxa8 Bxa8 8. b3 e6 9. bxc4 bxc4 10. Bxc4 Nf6 { Continue developing and recover c4 at the right moment. The open files favor pieces already in play. Watch for: Check rook exchanges all the way through: a missing rook is not a free bishop on a8. } *

[Event "Study line 04-05"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "A pin with ...Bg4"]
[Result "*"]

{ e3 and Bxc4 are enough to maintain a sound position. You can tolerate the pin while the king reaches safety. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. Nf3 Bg4 4. e3 Nf6 5. Bxc4 e6 6. O-O Be7 7. Nc3 c5 8. h3 Bh5 9. Qb3 Bxf3 10. gxf3 cxd4 { Use Nc3 and h3 only when it helps. If Black exchanges on f3, choose the recapture that suits the center and king safety. Watch for: h3 is not obligatory if it merely encourages a useful bishop exchange for Black. } *

[Event "Study line 05-01"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "Main line with ...Bf5"]
[Result "*"]

{ a4 restrains ...b5, e3 releases the bishop, and e4 later uses your central majority after development. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 dxc4 5. a4 Bf5 6. e3 e6 7. Bxc4 Bb4 8. O-O O-O 9. Qe2 Nbd7 10. e4 Bg6 { Support e4, develop the c1 bishop, and watch ...Bg6 or ...Bxc3. The d4-e4 center can advance only if the pieces behind it cooperate. Watch for: Do not confuse space with stability: ...Nxe4 may work tactically when the c3 knight is pinned. } *

[Event "Study line 05-02"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "The ...Bg4 alternative"]
[Result "*"]

{ The bishop pins f3 instead of controlling e4 from f5. You can still use the main development scheme. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 dxc4 5. a4 Bg4 6. e3 e6 7. Bxc4 Bb4 8. O-O Nbd7 9. Qb3 Qb6 10. Nd2 a5 { Meet pressure on d4 with active development. A timely h3 can ask the bishop to decide, but first ensure the central pawns are adequately defended. Watch for: After ...Bxf3, a queen recapture may abandon a tactical defense elsewhere. Count before recapturing. } *

[Event "Study line 05-03"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "Chebanenko: ...a6"]
[Result "*"]

{ e3 and b3 build a stable center while preparing to contest the long diagonal. Black's queenside expansion gains space but also creates targets. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 a6 5. e3 b5 6. b3 Bg4 7. Be2 e6 8. c5 Nbd7 9. O-O Be7 10. Ne1 Bf5 { Castle, finish development with Bb2, and challenge the center rather than racing the b-pawn without support. Watch for: cxb5 followed by a recapture can strengthen Black's central or queenside pawn mass. Calculate the structure before exchanging. } *

[Event "Study line 05-04"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "Early ...Bf5 before capturing"]
[Result "*"]

{ The queen eyes b7 after the bishop has left c8. This makes Black account for the queenside instead of completing a setup for free. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 Bf5 5. cxd5 cxd5 6. Qb3 Bc8 7. e4 Nxe4 8. Bb5+ Bd7 9. Qxd5 Nxc3 10. bxc3 Bxb5 { Develop e3 and Bd3 if the queen raid does not win anything concrete. Pressure is useful even when you do not capture the pawn. Watch for: Qxb7 can trap your queen or fall behind in development. The threat may be stronger than the capture. } *

[Event "Study line 05-05"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "An early ...e5 counterstrike"]
[Result "*"]

{ Accept the central pawn and recognize that the knight leap is Black's source of compensation. The center is now tactical, not a standard Slav setup. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 e5 5. dxe5 Ne4 6. Nxe4 dxe4 7. Qxd8+ Kxd8 8. Ng5 Be6 9. Nxe6+ fxe6 10. f4 exf3 { Develop actively and be willing to return a pawn to neutralize threats. Check queen checks and the c3 knight before choosing a capture. Watch for: Do not mix this ...e5 line with the Albin: the black c-pawn is on c6, and the knight is already on f6 before jumping. } *

[Event "Study line 05-06"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "Black captures before developing ...Nf6"]
[Result "*"]

{ e3 prepares Bxc4 while a4 challenges the supporting pawn chain. Black has spent several moves on pawns instead of developing the king side. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 dxc4 4. e3 b5 5. a4 e6 6. axb5 cxb5 7. b3 Nf6 8. bxc4 bxc4 9. Bxc4 Bd6 10. O-O O-O { Recover c4 when safe and bring the king to safety. Watch a possible ...b4 before placing the b1 knight on c3. Watch for: The move order differs from the main Slav, where Nc3 was already played before the capture. } *

[Event "Study line 06-01"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "Meran: the central counterstrike"]
[Result "*"]

{ White gains time against the f6 knight while Black attacks the center. This is a concrete line: e5 is a central reaction to ...c5, not a generic attacking move. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 e6 5. e3 Nbd7 6. Bd3 dxc4 7. Bxc4 b5 8. Bd3 a6 9. e4 c5 10. e5 cxd4 { Calculate captures on d4 and b5. Keep pieces active; the position can open before either side has completed the familiar quiet setup. Watch for: After 10.e5, stop and calculate Black's reply. This is a place to learn a branch rather than blindly finishing a ten-move script. } *

[Event "Study line 06-02"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "Anti-Meran with ...Bd6"]
[Result "*"]

{ Qc2 reinforces e4 while castling removes your king from the center. Both players retain choices about the central tension. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 e6 5. e3 Nbd7 6. Bd3 Bd6 7. O-O O-O 8. Qc2 e5 9. cxd5 cxd5 10. e4 exd4 { Re1, e4 and queenside development are candidates. If Black captures c4 and pushes ...b5, retreat the bishop and strike centrally when ready. Watch for: e4 can fail if ...dxe4 and a tactical knight capture leave you losing a central pawn. Count the sequence. } *

[Event "Study line 06-03"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "The early ...a6 Meran"]
[Result "*"]

{ Black delays the b8 knight and begins expansion. White castles before choosing a direct central confrontation. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 e6 5. e3 a6 6. Bd3 dxc4 7. Bxc4 b5 8. Bd3 c5 9. O-O Bb7 10. dxc5 Bxc5 { e4 is attractive if properly supported. Compare a quick e4 with keeping the center and improving the queen and rooks. Watch for: Do not assume every ...a6 line is identical: a knight on d7 changes tactical defenses and available breaks. } *

[Event "Study line 06-04"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "Black exchanges on c4 before ...Nbd7"]
[Result "*"]

{ Bxc4 and Bd3 accept that a developing bishop may have to move twice. Black also spent time on a capture and a pawn push. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 e6 5. e3 dxc4 6. Bxc4 b5 7. Bd3 Bb7 8. O-O a6 9. e4 c5 10. d5 exd5 { Use e4 once the central support is sufficient. Watch the b7 bishop's diagonal toward g2 after any central exchange. Watch for: Opening the long diagonal without checking g2 can turn a routine center push into a tactical error. } *

[Event "Study line 06-05"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "A Stonewall by ...f5"]
[Result "*"]

{ Black fixes a Stonewall and gives up control of e5. b3 prepares a development route for the c1 bishop. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 e6 4. Nc3 f5 5. e3 Nf6 6. Bd3 Bd6 7. O-O O-O 8. b3 Bd7 9. Ne2 Be8 10. Nf4 Qe7 { Bb2 or Ba3 may improve the bishop. Aim a knight at e5 and restrain ...g5 or ...Ne4 by controlling key entry squares. Watch for: If a black knight is already on e4, b3 may remove the only pawn defense of Nc3 and allow ...Nxc3. Verify that detail before using the bishop-development plan. } *

[Event "Study line 07-01"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "Tarrasch: the fianchetto setup"]
[Result "*"]

{ The bishop on g2 attacks central dark squares. Exchanging d4xc5 can leave Black with an isolated d5 pawn. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 c5 4. cxd5 exd5 5. g3 Nc6 6. Bg2 Nf6 7. O-O Be7 8. dxc5 Bxc5 9. Nc3 O-O 10. a3 a5 { Use a knight to restrain d4 and place rooks on the central files. Do not hurry to win the pawn at the expense of piece coordination. Watch for: ...d4 can gain time on Nc3. Calculate the advance before parking pieces on squares it attacks. } *

[Event "Study line 07-02"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "The ...c4 space-gaining alternative"]
[Result "*"]

{ White can also maintain the central tension. Black's choice between ...cxd4, ...c4 and waiting changes where your counterplay belongs. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 c5 4. cxd5 exd5 5. g3 Nc6 6. Bg2 Nf6 7. O-O Be7 8. Nc3 O-O 9. dxc5 Bxc5 10. a3 a5 { If ...c4 closes the queenside, a timely b3 can challenge the chain. If the center opens, the g2 bishop becomes stronger. Watch for: Do not use b3 before checking ...Bb4 and pressure on c3. A strategic break still needs tactical support. } *

[Event "Study line 07-03"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "Semi-Tarrasch with an isolated white pawn"]
[Result "*"]

{ The e-pawn recapture keeps active central space but leaves d4 isolated. Re1 supports active piece play and a possible d5 break. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 c5 5. cxd5 Nxd5 6. e3 Nc6 7. Bd3 cxd4 8. exd4 Be7 9. O-O O-O 10. Re1 Bd7 { Develop Bf4 or Bg5 when useful, bring the queen toward the center, and look for d5 when Black's pieces are uncoordinated. Watch for: Trading every minor piece can leave you defending a weak pawn in an endgame. Judge trades by activity, not by quantity. } *

[Event "Study line 07-04"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "An early ...Nf6-c5 move order"]
[Result "*"]

{ This is the same Semi-Tarrasch through an Indian move order. The choice of 3.Nf3 did not remove ...c5 from Black's options. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 d5 4. Nc3 c5 5. cxd5 Nxd5 6. e3 cxd4 7. exd4 Nxc3 8. bxc3 Qc7 9. Bb2 Be7 10. Bd3 O-O { Follow the development scheme when the position matches. If Black changes the recapture, inspect the pawn structure again. Watch for: A name on a database screen is less important than the actual recapture on d5. } *

[Event "Study line 08-01"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "Main choice: ...Nge7 and the queen trade"]
[Result "*"]

{ Nbd2-b3 attacks d4. e4 challenges the knight and allows an en-passant capture; Qxd8+ first is essential to understand the resulting queenless position. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 e5 3. dxe5 d4 4. Nf3 Nc6 5. Nbd2 Nge7 6. Nb3 Nf5 7. e4 dxe3 8. Qxd8+ Kxd8 9. fxe3 a5 10. Bd3 Bb4+ { Develop the bishops, bring the king to a safe useful square, and use the extra material without rushing. Your e-pawns are doubled, so assess activity as well as pawn count. Watch for: After e2-e4, ...dxe3 is en passant. Do not draw the pawn on e4 as if it survived that capture. } *

[Event "Study line 08-02"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "The pin: ...Bg4 and ...Qe7"]
[Result "*"]

{ a3 prevents ...Bb4+; h3 asks the bishop to choose. Qa4 creates queenside pressure while Black spends time placing the queen on e7. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 e5 3. dxe5 d4 4. Nf3 Nc6 5. Nbd2 Bg4 6. a3 Qe7 7. h3 Bh5 8. Qa4 O-O-O 9. g4 Bg6 10. Bg2 Kb8 { Meet castling long with useful queenside development and possibly b4. If Black regains e5, exchange accurately and complete development. Watch for: The f3 knight is pinned to the queen while the bishop stays on g4 or h5. Capturing with it can expose a queen capture. } *

[Event "Study line 08-03"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "...Be6 and the d4 blockade"]
[Result "*"]

{ The bishop on e6 supports active development. The fianchetto provides a safe king scheme while the b3 knight questions d4. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 e5 3. dxe5 d4 4. Nf3 Nc6 5. Nbd2 Be6 6. a3 Nge7 7. Nb3 Nf5 8. g3 Be7 9. h4 a5 10. Bf4 a4 { Bg2 and castling are sensible aims. Check whether an exchange on d4 opens lines against your king or improves your center. Watch for: Do not grab d4 if Black can exploit a pin, a check, or the loose e5 pawn with tempo. } *

[Event "Study line 08-04"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "...Bf5 allows Nb3 with tempo ideas"]
[Result "*"]

{ Nb3 prepares a capture on d4 that would hit Bf5. Meet the check by developing Bd2 and recapture on d2 with the queen. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 e5 3. dxe5 d4 4. Nf3 Nc6 5. Nbd2 Bf5 6. Nb3 Bb4+ 7. Bd2 Qe7 8. a3 Bxd2+ 9. Qxd2 Be4 10. O-O-O O-O-O { Finish development; do not assume that castling on either side is automatically safe. The queen on d2 helps you organize the position. Watch for: A line with the bishop on f5 is not identical to the ...Bg4 pin. Tactical defenses differ. } *

[Event "Study line 08-05"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "An early ...a5 restrains b4"]
[Result "*"]

{ The knight takes the central wedge instead of retreating passively from the a-pawn. The point of Nb3 was to make d4 a target. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 e5 3. dxe5 d4 4. Nf3 Nc6 5. Nbd2 a5 6. Nb3 a4 7. Nbxd4 Bc5 8. e3 Nge7 9. Be2 O-O 10. Nb5 Bd7 { Develop the remaining pieces and castle if safe. Black has spent time on the a-pawn and may seek compensation through open lines. Watch for: Check which knight can capture d4; write Nbxd4 when both knights could reach that square. } *

[Event "Study line 08-06"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "Warning only: the Lasker underpromotion trap"]
[Result "*"]

{ WARNING: losing example, not a White recommendation. This is a demonstration of what not to play. 4.e3 allows ...Bb4+; 6.Bxb4 ignores the passed pawn, and promotion to a knight comes with check. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 e5 3. dxe5 d4 4. e3 Bb4+ 5. Bd2 dxe3 6. Bxb4 exf2+ 7. Ke2 fxg1=N+ 8. Rxg1 Bg4+ 9. Ke1 Bxd1 10. Nc3 Bg4 { Avoid this entire mess with the recommended 4.Nf3. If you have already played e3, stop before capturing the bishop and examine checks and passed-pawn threats. Watch for: After ...fxg1=N+, the promoted knight attacks e2. Promotion to a queen would not create that same knight check. } *

[Event "Study line 09-01"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "Main reply: exchange and accept the structural trade"]
[Result "*"]

{ Black gives up bishops to damage your pawns. White accepts a broad center and the bishop pair; e3 supports development and the d4 square. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 Nc6 3. Nf3 Bg4 4. cxd5 Bxf3 5. gxf3 Qxd5 6. e3 e5 7. Nc3 Bb4 8. Bd2 Bxc3 9. bxc3 exd4 10. cxd4 Nge7 { Develop the f1 bishop and secure the king according to the open lines. The central pawns can restrict the knights, but must remain supported. Watch for: This structure is not a normal QGD. The g-file is open and the f-pawns are doubled; reassess king safety rather than castling by reflex. } *

[Event "Study line 09-02"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "Black develops ...Nf6"]
[Result "*"]

{ Black has chosen a solid setup with the c-pawn blocked. Ordinary development gives you a comfortable central position. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 Nc6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 e6 5. Bg5 Be7 6. e3 O-O 7. Rc1 h6 8. Bf4 Bd6 9. Bg3 Bxg3 10. hxg3 Qe7 { Bd3 and castling come first. A later cxd5 may lead to a structure where Black needs ...e5 to free the position. Watch for: Do not exchange automatically if keeping the tension restricts Black more effectively. } *

[Event "Study line 09-03"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "Black captures the c-pawn"]
[Result "*"]

{ Recover with the bishop while accepting that Black can build an e5 center. You should compare central control, not just the opening label. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 Nc6 3. Nf3 dxc4 4. e3 e5 5. Bxc4 Bb4+ 6. Nc3 exd4 7. exd4 Nf6 8. O-O O-O 9. h3 h6 10. Bf4 Bd6 { Castle and use Nc3 to increase pressure on the center. Calculate d5 only when it gains something concrete. Watch for: A premature d5 may surrender key squares and help Black's knights. } *

[Event "Study line 09-04"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "Early ...e5 in the center"]
[Result "*"]

{ This resembles the Albin but has a different move order. The pawn wedge still deserves attention, and Nbd2 is a useful developing idea. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 Nc6 3. Nf3 e5 4. dxe5 d4 5. a3 Nge7 6. b4 Ng6 7. Bb2 a5 8. b5 Ncxe5 9. Nxe5 Nxe5 10. Bxd4 Nxc4 { Use the Albin booklet as a structural guide, while checking the exact position and whether every transposition really matches. Watch for: The same pawn structure can occur with a different side to move. Never add a free tempo when memorizing a transposition. } *

[Event "Study line 10-01"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "Baltic: answer ...Bf5 concretely"]
[Result "*"]

{ The rook recapture keeps your king and queen safe. Black recovers d5 but has exchanged an active bishop for your knight. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 Bf5 3. cxd5 Bxb1 4. Rxb1 Qxd5 5. Nf3 Nf6 6. a3 Nc6 7. e3 e5 8. dxe5 Qxd1+ 9. Kxd1 Ng4 10. Ke2 Ngxe5 { Develop e3 and Bd3 and bring the king to safety. The b-file rook may later pressure b7. Watch for: The rook on b1 does not mean you must start a queenside attack immediately. Finish development. } *

[Event "Study line 10-02"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "Baltic: Black recaptures with ...e6"]
[Result "*"]

{ White accepts another central pawn while letting Black develop. The extra pawn must be balanced against the time spent capturing. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 Bf5 3. cxd5 e6 4. dxe6 Bxe6 5. Nf3 Nf6 6. Nc3 Bb4 7. Bd2 c5 8. a3 cxd4 9. axb4 dxc3 10. Bxc3 Qe7 { Use e3 or g3 as appropriate, develop the b1 knight, and castle. Be willing to return material if that eliminates a serious initiative. Watch for: Clinging to a pawn while leaving the king in the center can reverse the evaluation quickly. } *

[Event "Study line 10-03"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "Marshall Defense: ...Nf6"]
[Result "*"]

{ e4 builds a broad center with tempo against the knight. You gain space without chasing a queen around the board. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 Nf6 3. cxd5 Nxd5 4. e4 Nf6 5. Nc3 e5 6. dxe5 Qxd1+ 7. Kxd1 Ng4 8. Ke1 Bc5 9. f4 Nc6 10. Nd5 O-O { Develop Nf3, bring out the bishops, and castle. Be ready for ...e5 or ...c5 to question the center. Watch for: Do not push e5 unless it improves your position; the existing center is already an asset. } *

[Event "Study line 10-04"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "Symmetrical counter: ...c5"]
[Result "*"]

{ Central exchanges produce an open position. e4 can gain space while attacking the knight on d5. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 c5 3. cxd5 Nf6 4. Nf3 cxd4 5. Nxd4 Nxd5 6. e4 Nf6 7. Bb5+ Bd7 8. Bxd7+ Qxd7 9. Nc3 e5 10. Nc2 Qxd1+ { Develop quickly and calculate queen checks before chasing knights further. Open files reward a castled king and connected rooks. Watch for: Do not assume all pawn captures are forced. An intermediate developing move can be stronger. } *

[Event "Study line 10-05"]
[Site "Original 1.d4 course"]
[Date "2026.09.04"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "An early ...Bg4"]
[Result "*"]

{ Nc3 and Nf3 are useful developing moves. Black's bishop is active, but the center and b7 can become targets. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 Bg4 3. Nc3 e6 4. Nf3 Nf6 5. Ne5 Bf5 6. Qb3 Nc6 7. Qa4 Bb4 8. Nxc6 Bxc3+ 9. bxc3 Qd7 10. f3 Qxc6 { Choose e3 and a safe king setup, or question the bishop if there is a concrete gain. Use the actual central structure to select a plan. Watch for: Do not play f3 casually: it takes the natural f3 square from the knight and weakens the king. } *

