{ Here White deferred c4 and took e4. The center is locked, so the right wing for play depends on piece placement and possible pawn breaks. } 1. d4 c5 2. d5 e5 3. e4 d6 4. Nf3 Nf6 5. Nc3 Be7 6. Be2 O-O 7. O-O Ne8 8. Bd3 Bd7 9. a3 Bg4 10. h3 Bxf3 { Prepare a queenside expansion or central undermining move. c4 is optional later; it should improve control rather than merely imitate another opening. Watch for: Do not assume a locked center makes kingside pawn advances safe forever. A break can open files suddenly. } *

