{ White castles before confronting the center. d5 gains space and asks how Black will organize ...e5. } 1. d4 f5 2. g3 Nf6 3. Bg2 g6 4. Nf3 Bg7 5. O-O O-O 6. c4 d6 7. Nc3 c6 8. d5 cxd5 9. Nxd5 e5 10. Bg5 Nc6 { Keep central control and watch ...Qe8 or ...Na6. A timely e4 can be strong, but its tactical support must be real. Watch for: The black f-pawn has already moved; opening the center may expose either king depending on development. } *

