{ d5 gains space and leads to a Benoni-like structure without requiring the standard ...e6 exchange immediately. } 1. d4 g6 2. c4 Bg7 3. Nf3 c5 4. d5 d6 5. g3 Nf6 6. Bg2 O-O 7. O-O Re8 8. Re1 a6 9. Nc3 e6 10. Nd2 exd5 { Castle, develop Nc3 and consider e4. Black's ...e6 and ...b5 breaks must be monitored. Watch for: Without an exchange on d5, the pawn structure differs from the Modern Benoni main line. Reassess before using a memorized plan. } *

